---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Layout from "@/layouts/Project.astro";
import Heading from "@/components/ui/Heading.astro";
import Text from "@/components/ui/Text.astro";
import ProjectContnetWrapper from "@/components/ui/ProjectContnetWrapper.astro";
import SwiperSlider from "@/components/ui/SwiperSlider.astro";
import FlowMap from "@/components/ui/FlowMap.astro";
import { loadImages } from "@/utils/loadImages";

import logoImg from "@/assets/logo/alfalogo-min.png";
import searchVariantsImg from "@/assets/alfaHomeDecor/search_form_variants.png";
import refundFlowUrl from "@/assets/alfaFlow/refund_flow.svg?url";
import refundVoluntaryMapImg from "@/assets/alfaFlow/refund_voluntary_map.webp";
import exchangeMapImg from "@/assets/alfaFlow/exchange_map.webp";

const title = "Alfa Bank - Alfa Travel";
const years = "2023-2025";

const imageModulesHomeMobile = import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>(
  "/src/assets/alfaHomeMobile/*.{jpeg,jpg,png,gif}"
);
const imageModulesHomeDesktop = import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>(
  "/src/assets/alfaHomeDesktop/*.{jpeg,jpg,png,gif}"
);

const sliderImagesHomeMobile = await loadImages(imageModulesHomeMobile);
const sliderImagesHomeDesktop = await loadImages(imageModulesHomeDesktop);
---

<Layout {title} {years} logo={logoImg} titleLink="https://alfabank.ru/travel/">
  {/* ---------- 1. CONTEXT ---------- */}
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Text>
      Alfa-Bank is the largest private bank in Russia. Alfa Travel is its
      travel platform: flights, hotels, trains and holidays, booked and paid
      for inside the customer's bank account.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      I was the travel department's first designer and its only one for two
      years, working on desktop and on the mobile webview inside the bank's app.
    </Text>

    {/* ---------- 2. ROLE & SCOPE ---------- */}
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">My Role</Heading>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      Sole designer for the travel department. I owned flights, hotels, trains
      and holidays from research and flows to final UI and handoff, on both
      desktop and mobile.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">Over two years I shipped:</Text>
    <ul
      class="mb-[1.562rem] list-none pl-0 text-[1.062rem] leading-[1.6]"
    >
      <li>🔁 Self-service ticket refunds and exchanges, desktop and mobile</li>
      <li>🏠 The travel homepage redesign and its recommendation blocks</li>
      <li>🏨 The hotel booking flow, designed from scratch</li>
      <li>✈️ Flight search and booking, rebuilt on the bank's components</li>
      <li>🔍 Search across all four products: flights, hotels, trains, holidays</li>
      <li>
        👤 The personal account where users manage trips and tickets, extended
        and rebuilt on components
      </li>
      <li>🧳 Ancillaries and the smaller fixes across the booking funnel</li>
      <li>🧪 A/B tests on key steps of the booking funnel</li>
      <li>
        🔎 Competitor analysis and a reference library of travel homepages, in
        Russia and abroad
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      This case looks closely at two of them, refunds and exchanges and the
      homepage redesign, because they show the constraints I had to work with.
    </Text>

    {/* ---------- 3. PLATFORM HISTORY ---------- */}
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold"
      >The Platform I Inherited</Heading
    >
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      Alfa Travel started as another travel company's white label, repainted in
      the bank's style. As it grew, the bank wanted a product of its own, and
      that is when I was hired: to rebuild it on Alfa Bank's components, which
      was a hard requirement, on top of the partner's API.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      That gave us control and more work at once. Every screen was now ours to
      draw and had to fit the design system, and desktop barely existed when I
      arrived.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>

  {/* ---------- 4. DEEP DIVE: REFUNDS & EXCHANGES ---------- */}
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading class="mt-[100px] mb-[10px] text-[28px]" level={2} weight="semibold"
      >Refunds & Exchanges</Heading
    >
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">The Problem</Heading>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      As the product grew, so did the number of requests to support. Refunds
      and exchanges were handled by people, and support could not keep up.
      Customers waited a long time for an answer, and with travel that hurts:
      a delayed reply about a flight is not a small thing for the person
      waiting.
    </Text>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">The Approach</Heading>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      We built a self-service flow inside the personal account. The customer
      picks the ticket, states the reason, selects the passengers and attaches
      the documents themselves. From there the request is either checked
      automatically or handed to support as a ready task, with no back and
      forth to find out what the customer wants.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      The visual work here is small. It is built on the bank's design system,
      and most of the effort went into the logic: what happens at each step,
      where the flow waits for the airline, where it waits for the customer,
      and where it ends.
    </Text>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">How It Works</Heading>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <div
    class="mt-[40px] mb-[25px] overflow-x-auto scroll-smooth no-scrollbar
      px-[var(--container-padding)] lg:px-[var(--offset-left)]"
    style=`--offset-left: calc(50vw - (1200px/2))`
    tabindex="0"
    role="region"
    aria-label="Ticket refund flow diagram"
  >
    <img
      src={refundFlowUrl}
      alt="The ticket refund flow from start to finish, with the two points where the journey can end"
      class="max-w-[none] h-[458px]"
    />
  </div>
  <Text class="mb-[100px] max-w-[452px] m-auto text-center text-[15px] italic">
    The refund journey end to end. Two of the steps can close it: the airline
    refusing, and the customer declining the quote or letting the timer expire.
  </Text>
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">Every Screen</Heading>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      The refund as it was designed and built, with every screen and every
      branch in place.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <FlowMap
    src={refundVoluntaryMapImg}
    fullHref="/flows/refund_voluntary_map_full.webp"
    alt="The full refund flow: every screen and branch, connected with arrows"
  />
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">Exchange</Heading>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      An exchange is harder than a refund, because the customer has to say
      exactly what they want to replace. A booking can have several flights
      and connections, and the customer may change only one leg, only the part
      before or after a connection, or the whole route. It can be for one
      passenger or several.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      That is where the corner cases live. A child cannot be separated from the
      adult they fly with, so a change for the adult has to carry the child
      with it. The main route is what you see below; the branches around it
      handle the rest.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <FlowMap
    src={exchangeMapImg}
    fullHref="/flows/exchange_map_full.webp"
    alt="The full ticket exchange flow: every screen and branch, connected with arrows"
  />
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">Outcome</Heading>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      Most refund and exchange cases now close through the self-service flow.
      The corner cases still reach support, but as a prepared task rather than
      a conversation. Support requests about tickets dropped by <b>74.1%</b>.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      The flow went through many iterations and rounds of sign-off. What
      shipped is the version that could pass, not every choice in it is the
      one I would make on my own, and it works.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>

  {/* ---------- 5. DEEP DIVE: HOMEPAGE ---------- */}
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading class="mt-[100px] mb-[10px] text-[28px]" level={2} weight="semibold"
      >Homepage Redesign</Heading
    >
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      Most customers live in the bank's mobile app, where Alfa Travel runs as a
      webview, so the mobile homepage is the main one. Desktop is a second entry
      point on the website.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      I started with calls to the heads of the travel business, a competitor
      analysis and a reference library of travel homepages from Russia and
      abroad. The bento grid came from the bank's main site, and I agreed every
      step with its designers.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      The page had to carry banners, because promos and partnerships bring
      revenue, so I prepared grid options for different numbers of them. Around
      that I added popular hotels and popular destinations, and later the
      events guide, tied to where the customer is searching or where they live.
    </Text>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">Craft</Heading>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      The homepage is the one piece that is almost not on the design system.
      The hard requirements were short: the bank's buttons, inputs and
      typeface, the bento grid at least in part so the page matches the bank's
      main site, and a list of what had to be on the page, the banners
      included. Everything else on the page is my own visual work.
    </Text>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">Sign-off</Heading>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      Alfa is a large corporation, so nothing shipped on one person's decision.
      Each piece went through several rounds of sign-off: inside the travel
      department, then with the design team and my team lead, who ran several
      designers. For the important work, like the homepage, it also went to
      the business side and up to C-level.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      That is where most of the trade-offs came from. The result had to work
      for all of them, not only for me, and some choices on the page are the
      ones that could pass rather than the ones I would draw with a free hand.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">Mobile</Heading>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <div class="mt-[40px] mb-[25px]">
    <SwiperSlider
      sliderImages={sliderImagesHomeMobile}
      initialWidth="224px"
      video={true}
    />
  </div>
  <Text class="mb-[100px] max-w-[452px] m-auto text-center text-[15px] italic">
    Entry screen, promo entry, homepage in light and dark, the events guide and
    a place card.
  </Text>
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">Desktop</Heading>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <div class="mt-[40px] mb-[100px]">
    <SwiperSlider sliderImages={sliderImagesHomeDesktop} initialWidth="300px" />
  </div>

  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">Explorations</Heading>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      The search form was the part everyone had an opinion about, so it went
      through the most versions: tabs against segmented control, a light hero
      against a dark and a photo one, the form inside a card against the form on
      the background.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
  <Image
    src={searchVariantsImg}
    alt="Five explored versions of the travel search form"
    width="330"
    densities={[1, 2]}
    class="m-auto mb-[100px]"
  />

  {/* ---------- 8. OTHER WORK ---------- */}
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading class="mt-[100px] mb-[10px] text-[28px]" level={2} weight="semibold"
      >Also Shipped</Heading
    >
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      Next to the two projects above, I designed the hotel booking flow from
      scratch, kept improving the personal account where people manage their
      trips and tickets, and worked on search for all four products: flights,
      hotels, trains and holidays. That included multi-city routes, search
      history and passenger validation.
    </Text>
    <Text class="mb-[1.562rem]">
      I also ran A/B tests on the steps that bring revenue directly: the choice
      of cabin class and the ancillaries.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>

  {/* ---------- 9. MENTORING ---------- */}
  <ProjectContnetWrapper>
    <Heading level={2} size="xl" weight="semibold">Mentoring</Heading>
    <Text class="mb-[50px]">
      When a middle designer joined the team, I onboarded them: distributed
      tasks and helped them understand the project and the design system.
    </Text>
  </ProjectContnetWrapper>
</Layout>
